Compared with Group I, the Group II elements:

1. are **harder and denser** (Group I metals are soft, can be cut with a knife and have low densities; Li, Na and K float on water);
2. have **higher melting and boiling points** (for example Na melts at about 98 $^\circ$C while Mg melts at about 650 $^\circ$C), because each atom gives two electrons to the metallic bond.

Suggest a reagent which could be used to convert

(i) ethanol to ethene;

(ii) ethanol to ethyl ethanoate. *[2 marks]*

State the raw materials used in the production of

(i) ammonia;

(ii) nitric acid. *[1 mark each]*

Ammonia and nitric acid are used for the production of fertilizers. State a fertilizer produced from

(i) ammonia;

(ii) nitric acid. *[2 marks]*

The binding energy for electrons in a metal is:

1. also known as the work function;
2. the difference between the photon energy and the maximum K.E. for an emitted electron ;
3. photon energy on tricking the metal.

A $12.0\ \mathrm{pF}$ capacitor with air between its plates is charged through a p.d. of $4.00 \times 10^{2}\ \mathrm{V}$. The capacitor is then disconnected from the supply. A material of relative permittivity $4.00$ is then inserted so that it fills the region between the plates completely.

1. This will cause the p.d. between the plates to acquire a value of $1.00 \times 10^{2}\ \mathrm{V}$;
2. This causes the charge stored in the capacitor to decrease as charge flow between the plates must take place, in order for the p.d. to decrease;
3. The new value for the p.d. between the plates is $2.00 \times 10^{2}\ \mathrm{V}$.
